<title>USA: USDA economist sees some U.S. acreage shift in '97.</title>
<headline>USDA economist sees some U.S. acreage shift in '97.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-12-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Agriculture Department chief economist Keith Collins said there will likely be some acreage shifts in the United States in the coming year due to a combination of weather and greater planting flexibility under the new farm bill.</p>
<p>&quot;There will be some changes,&quot; said Collins, who was participating in an interview with Agriculture Secretary Dan Glickman.</p>
<p>He said there will likely be more winter wheat acreage, especially in southern areas that were hit by drought this past year, and more corn acreage in areas where bad weather delayed planting.</p>
<p>He predicted soybean acreage would be about the same as 1996 and said cotton and rice acreage could go down &quot;a bit.&quot;</p>
</text>
